// Water you can name.
//
// Water is hard to write for the same reason a sky is: what makes it look right is a set of numbers
// nobody can guess.  Nobody knows what wavelength they want, or what exponent sharpens a crest.
// Everybody knows what a calm morning on a lake looks like, and what choppy looks like.
//
// So this library holds water by the state of it.  Take a surface and a water to go in it:
//
//     import 'water' { Water, SeaWater }
//
//     object Water(120, 200, 'breezy') { material SeaWater  translate Z 90 }
//
// **The surface does not come with a material, and that is deliberate.**  What a body of water looks
// like is mostly its material -- how dark it is, how much it mirrors, what it lets through -- so
// leaving it out would be leaving out the half that matters.  Pick one of `SeaWater`, `LakeWater` or
// `PoolWater` below, or write your own from them.
//
// **On real geometry rather than a lit lie.**  The surface here is an isosurface: the waves are
// actually there.  The silhouette against the horizon is as rough as the middle of the picture, the
// shadows are wave-shaped, and what the water refracts is bent by the slope it really has.  Tilting
// the normal of a flat plane -- a `normal` block -- gets a similar look head-on for far less work and
// gives it all away at the horizon, where a flat plane stays a straight line.
//
// **The price, which is worth knowing before the first render.**  Real waves are real detail, and
// detail at a distance aliases.  Left to one sample a pixel, water going away from the camera turns
// to grey mush around the horizon -- each pixel is averaging some normals that mirror the sky and
// some that look into the dark, and the average of those is neither.  Measured on a horizon-filling
// ocean at 400x300: 1.5 seconds with no anti-aliasing and a mush band, 31 seconds at `-a adaptive:3`
// and clean.  **Water wants anti-aliasing.**  That is not a fault in the water; it is the honest cost
// of the waves being there, and the same bill comes due for any surface with fine detail in it.
//
// **Give it something to reflect.**  Water is mostly a mirror, and a mirror in an empty room shows an
// empty room.  A flat background colour makes water look like paint, whatever else is right about it.
// A sky with structure -- a gradient, or better one of the skies from the `daylight` library -- is
// what turns the surface into water.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The states, from a dead calm to a gale.  Each is four numbers: how tall the swell is, how far apart
// the crests are, how sharply peaked they get, and how far they lean.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// How tall the swell stands, in scene units, crest to trough.
//
// **These are set by steepness, not by eye.**  What decides whether a surface reads as water is the
// ratio of a wave's height to its length, and the first set of numbers here was chosen by looking at
// one render from one camera: they came out between one and three percent, where real waves run to
// about one in seven before they break.  Water five times too shallow does not read as calm water, it
// reads as a rumpled sheet, and no amount of adjusting the material repairs it.  Against the lengths
// below these give a steepness of about 0.006 at 'glassy' rising to 0.11 at 'rough'.
function WaterCrest(state) -> number {
    switch (state) {
        case 'glassy' { return 0.022 }
        case 'calm' { return 0.16 }
        case 'breezy' { return 0.62 }
        case 'choppy' { return 1.60 }
        case 'rough' { return 5.20 }
        default { return 0.16 }
    }
}

// How far apart the crests run.  Longer with a bigger sea, which is what makes a rough sea read as
// large rather than merely as noisy: waves that grow taller without growing longer look like a scale
// model of themselves.
function WaterLength(state) -> number {
    switch (state) {
        case 'glassy' { return 3.5 }
        case 'calm' { return 7 }
        case 'breezy' { return 14 }
        case 'choppy' { return 20 }
        case 'rough' { return 46 }
        default { return 7 }
    }
}

// How sharply the crests peak.  This is the number that separates water from a bedsheet.
//
// A plain sine is symmetric -- as round at the top as at the bottom -- and reads as a swimming pool
// however tall it is made.  Real swell is trochoidal: peaked above, broad below, because the water at
// a crest is moving forward and bunches up there.  Raising a rectified sine to a power does the same
// thing to the shape: at 1 it is the plain sine, and the higher it goes the more the crests draw up
// into ridges with flats between them.
//
// Sharpening is only half of a trochoid, though: the other half is that the crest leans over in the
// direction it travels, and that is what `WaterLean` below does.  The two are separate knobs because
// they are separate effects -- how *peaked* the wave is, and how *asymmetric*.
function WaterSharpness(state) -> number {
    switch (state) {
        case 'glassy' { return 1.0 }
        case 'calm' { return 1.4 }
        case 'breezy' { return 2.2 }
        case 'choppy' { return 2.8 }
        case 'rough' { return 3.4 }
        default { return 1.4 }
    }
}

// How far a crest leans forward, as a fraction of a wavelength shifted at the top of the wave.
//
// **This is what makes swell trochoidal rather than merely peaked**, and it is the one shape property
// a height written as `y - f(x, z)` looks unable to have -- every point being directly above where it
// started.  The way round it is that an implicit surface need not be a graph: shear the field's own
// horizontal argument by its height, `f(x - lean * y, z)`, and the surface leans.  Nothing about the
// renderer changes; it is the same marched isosurface.
//
// Measured on a single train of crest 1.6 and wavelength 20, solving the implicit equation directly:
// the crest height stays at 1.6000 exactly while the two faces diverge, the ratio of back slope to
// front going 1.00, 0.68, 0.43 as the shear rises.  That is a trochoid's signature -- steep in front,
// broad behind, and no shorter for it.
//
// Given as a fraction of a wavelength so that it means the same thing at every sea state, since a
// shear in bare units would lean a ripple far harder than a swell.  Kept below about a third: past
// that the implicit surface stops leaning and starts folding into overlapping sheets, which is not a
// breaking wave but a mess.  A real breaking wave wants a different surface altogether.
function WaterLean(state) -> number {
    switch (state) {
        case 'glassy' { return 0 }
        case 'calm' { return 0.10 }
        case 'breezy' { return 0.18 }
        case 'choppy' { return 0.26 }
        case 'rough' { return 0.32 }
        default { return 0.10 }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The wave field itself.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The height of the water above its rest level at the point a field is asking about.
//
// This is a single expression because it has to be: a function called from inside a surface's own
// function may only be one, since the surface needs the slope of what it is given and a slope is
// worked out from the expression rather than run for.
//
// Five terms, each a wave train crossing the others at an angle.  The first two carry the swell and
// are sharpened; the last three are ripples, left as plain sines because ripples really are about
// symmetric and sharpening them only makes them expensive.  The angles are deliberately unrelated --
// nothing here is a multiple of anything else -- so the pattern does not come out looking woven.
//
// **Each swell term is a sharpened wave mixed back with its own plain one, and the mixing is not
// decoration.**  Sharpening a crest flattens the trough, necessarily: a number near nought raised to a
// power above one is much nearer nought, over a broad stretch either side of the lowest point.  Pure
// sharpening therefore paid for its ridges with dead-flat plateaus between them, which showed at
// 'rough' as smooth leaf-shaped patches lying in the troughs and looked like a fault in the renderer.
// Keeping a third of each term unsharpened leaves the crests drawn up and gives the troughs their
// curve back.
// The shear is applied to the two swell terms and not to the ripples.  Ripples are small enough that
// leaning them buys nothing visible and costs the marcher the same as leaning the swell does; and
// riding as they do on a surface that is itself already leaning, they are carried along by it.
function WaterHeight(crest, length, sharpness, variant, shear) -> number {
    return crest * 0.62 * (0.68 * pow(0.5 + 0.5 * sin((x * 0.92 + z * 0.39 - shear * y) / length * 6.283 + variant), sharpness)
                         + 0.32 * (0.5 + 0.5 * sin((x * 0.92 + z * 0.39 - shear * y) / length * 6.283 + variant)))
         + crest * 0.30 * (0.68 * pow(0.5 + 0.5 * sin((x * -0.42 + z * 0.91 - shear * y) / length * 9.100 + variant * 2.3), sharpness)
                         + 0.32 * (0.5 + 0.5 * sin((x * -0.42 + z * 0.91 - shear * y) / length * 9.100 + variant * 2.3)))
         + crest * 0.07 * sin((x * 0.71 + z * -0.70) / length * 31.4 + variant * 3.7)
         + crest * 0.035 * sin((x * 0.26 + z * 0.97) / length * 67.0 + variant * 5.1)
         + crest * 0.018 * sin((x * -0.87 + z * -0.49) / length * 121.0 + variant * 7.3)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The surface.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A body of water, lying in the X/Z plane with its rest level at y = 0 and centered on the origin.
//
// `across` and `along` are its extent in X and Z.  Make them larger than the picture needs rather
// than smaller: the box costs almost nothing to enlarge -- an ocean reaching the horizon measured 1.5
// seconds against 1.4 for a pond, since a ray finds the surface just as quickly either way -- and a
// box cut too small ends in a straight edge in mid-water, which reads as a cliff.
//
// `variant` shifts every wave train's phase, so two bodies of water in one scene are not the same
// water twice.
primitive Water(across, along, state = 'calm', variant = 1) -> isosurface {
    crest = WaterCrest(state)
    length = WaterLength(state)
    sharpness = WaterSharpness(state)

    // The lean is a fraction of a wavelength at the crest, so turning it into a shear on `y` means
    // dividing by how tall the crest is: a shift of `lean` wavelengths over a rise of `crest`.
    shear = WaterLean(state) * length / crest

    // The box has to hold the waves, and the sharpened terms all sum at a crest, so the room left
    // above is the sum of their weights rather than the tallest of them.  Below is only as deep as
    // the trough, which the rectified terms never take past nought.
    return isosurface {
        function { y - WaterHeight(crest, length, sharpness, variant, shear) }
        bounded by
            [-across / 2, -crest * 0.2, -along / 2],
            [across / 2, crest * 1.1, along / 2]
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The waters.  Each is a whole material, meant to be handed to the surface above.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// **Why these mostly transmit and mostly reflect at once, which sounds contradictory.**  Water does
// both, and which one you see depends entirely on the angle you see it at: looking straight down into
// a pool you see the bottom, and looking along the surface at the far shore you see the sky.  The
// renderer works that out for itself -- a material that both reflects and transmits gets its two
// contributions weighed by the angle, so the same water is a window underfoot and a mirror at the
// horizon with nothing said here to arrange it.  It is the one place where writing down two things
// that seem to disagree gives the right answer at every angle in between.

// Deep water: dark, blue-green, and lively.  The pigment barely shows -- most of what a sea looks
// like arrives from the sky above it -- but what there is of it wants to be dark, since a bright
// pigment is what makes water read as paint.
// **The clarity is what makes it a sea rather than a snowfield, and it took a render to see why.**
// Looking down into water, you see whatever lies below; a wave face turned toward the camera is
// mostly a window rather than a mirror, because that is what the angle decides.  With nothing below
// an open sea, the ray that goes down carries on for ever and comes back with the sky -- so the water
// showed sky whichever way it was facing, at every angle, and came out a uniform pale white.  It read
// as fresh snow.
//
// Clarity is the honest fix rather than putting a floor in: light fades over distance travelled
// through the substance, by Beer's law, so a ray heading down into deep water is simply absorbed and
// comes back with nothing.  That is what deep water *is*.  Nine units to fade to a third is dark
// enough to read as depth at the scale these wavelengths are written in.
//
// `filter` is a fraction and not a color -- how fully the pigment tints what passes through -- so the
// blue-green of deep water comes from the pigment above.
//
// **Absorbing alone overshot, and the ambient is standing in for what is missing.**  Set to absorb
// hard, the sea went past deep and came out nearly black -- convincing oil, unconvincing water.  Real
// water is not dark because light goes into it and is lost; it is blue-green because light goes in,
// *scatters*, and some of it comes back up.  Nothing here traces that: it would want a scattering
// medium inside the surface, which is real work and dear to render.  A little ambient and a longer
// clarity stand in for the light that ought to come back, which is a fudge and worth knowing is one.
SeaWater = material {
    pigment [0.025, 0.105, 0.125]
    ambient 0.09
    diffuse 0.10
    specular 1
    shininess 900
    reflective 1
    transparency 0.88
    interior {
        index of refraction Water
        clarity 24
        filter 1
    }
}

// Fresh water over a bed near enough to see: browner, and letting more through, so what is underneath
// does most of the talking.
LakeWater = material {
    pigment [0.045, 0.070, 0.050]
    ambient 0.03
    diffuse 0.10
    specular 1
    shininess 700
    reflective 0.85
    transparency 0.93
    interior { index of refraction Water }
}

// Clear water in a tiled pool: bright, clean, and almost entirely a window.  This is the one where
// what lies beneath matters most, so give it something worth refracting.
PoolWater = material {
    pigment [0.10, 0.30, 0.34]
    ambient 0.05
    diffuse 0.12
    specular 1
    shininess 800
    reflective 0.7
    transparency 0.95
    interior { index of refraction Water }
}
